Names are lowercase: code, files, keys. CAPS is shell folklore, not plan9.
Prose is in sentence case. A capital opens a sentence or a heading, the
rest is lowercase; no title case. CAPS mid-line for emphasis stays: that
is stress, not a name. A leaf written lowercase is re-cased when it is
next edited.

The check, on leaves, markdown and a page's text, catches title case:
three capitalized words in a row. A sentence left lowercase it cannot
tell from one not yet re-cased. A `~/` line is spelling not ours to fix:

  grep -nE '[A-Z][a-z]+ [A-Z][a-z]+ [A-Z][a-z]+' \
    $(find . -type f ! -name '*.*') | grep -v '~/'
